# Environment Configuration

## Environment File Structure

### File Naming

```
.env.local          # Local overrides (gitignored)
.env.development    # Development environment
.env.staging        # Staging environment
.env.production     # Production environment
.env.example        # Example file (committed)
```

### Environment File Priority (Next.js)

1. `.env.local` (always loaded, except in test)
2. `.env.development`, `.env.production`, `.env.staging` (based on NODE_ENV)
3. `.env` (default, always loaded)

## Environment Variable Naming

### Naming Convention

- Use `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`
- Prefix with app/service name if needed: `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`
- Use descriptive names: `DATABASE_URL`, `API_SECRET_KEY`
- Group related vars: `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` for client-side

### Naming Examples

```bash
# ✅ Good
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://...
API_SECRET_KEY=secret123
SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=xxx

# ❌ Bad
apiUrl=https://api.example.com
db_url=postgresql://...
secret=secret123
```

## Next.js Environment Variables

### Client-Side Variables

```bash
# Must be prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_ to be exposed to browser
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=Vamenta
```

### Server-Side Variables

```bash
# No prefix - only available on server
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://...
API_SECRET_KEY=secret123
SESSION_SECRET=secret456
```

## TypeScript Environment Types

### Environment Type Definition

```typescript
// env.d.ts or types/env.d.ts
declare namespace NodeJS {
  interface ProcessEnv {
    // Next.js public variables
    readonly NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL: string;
    readonly NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME: string;
    
    // Server-side variables
    readonly DATABASE_URL: string;
    readonly API_SECRET_KEY: string;
    readonly SESSION_SECRET: string;
    
    // Optional variables
    readonly SUPABASE_URL?: string;
    readonly SUPABASE_ANON_KEY?: string;
  }
}
```

## Environment Validation

### Runtime Validation with Zod

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const envSchema = z.object({
  NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL: z.string().url(),
  NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME: z.string().min(1),
  DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
  API_SECRET_KEY: z.string().min(32),
  SESSION_SECRET: z.string().min(32),
  NODE_ENV: z.enum(['development', 'production', 'test']).default('development'),
});

export type Env = z.infer<typeof envSchema>;

function validateEnv(): Env {
  try {
    return envSchema.parse(process.env);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof z.ZodError) {
      console.error('❌ Invalid environment variables:', error.flatten().fieldErrors);
      throw new Error('Invalid environment variables');
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

export const env = validateEnv();
```

### Validation on App Start

```typescript
// app/layout.tsx or app initialization
import { env } from '@/shared/config/env';

// Validate on app start
if (typeof window === 'undefined') {
  // Server-side validation
  console.log('Environment validated:', {
    nodeEnv: env.NODE_ENV,
    hasApiUrl: !!env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL,
  });
}
```

## Environment Config Module

### Centralized Config

```typescript
// shared/config/env.ts
import { z } from 'zod';

const envSchema = z.object({
  NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL: z.string().url(),
  NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME: z.string().min(1),
  DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
  API_SECRET_KEY: z.string().min(32),
  NODE_ENV: z.enum(['development', 'production', 'test']).default('development'),
});

function validateEnv() {
  try {
    return envSchema.parse(process.env);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof z.ZodError) {
      const missingVars = Object.keys(error.flatten().fieldErrors);
      throw new Error(
        `Missing or invalid environment variables: ${missingVars.join(', ')}`
      );
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

export const env = validateEnv();

// Export typed config
export const config = {
  api: {
    url: env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL,
  },
  app: {
    name: env.NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME,
    isDevelopment: env.NODE_ENV === 'development',
    isProduction: env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
    isTest: env.NODE_ENV === 'test',
  },
  database: {
    url: env.DATABASE_URL,
  },
  secrets: {
    apiKey: env.API_SECRET_KEY,
  },
} as const;
```

## Using Environment Variables

### Client-Side Usage

```typescript
// ✅ Good - use typed config
import { config } from '@/shared/config/env';

export function apiClient() {
  return axios.create({
    baseURL: config.api.url,
  });
}

// ❌ Bad - direct process.env access
export function apiClient() {
  return axios.create({
    baseURL: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL, // Not type-safe
  });
}
```

### Server-Side Usage

```typescript
// app/api/events/route.ts
import { config } from '@/shared/config/env';

export async function GET() {
  // Use config.secrets.apiKey (only available on server)
  const response = await fetch(config.api.url, {
    headers: {
      Authorization: `Bearer ${config.secrets.apiKey}`,
    },
  });

  return response.json();
}
```

## .env.example File

### Example File Template

```bash
# API Configuration
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=Vamenta

# Database
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname

# Secrets (never commit actual values)
API_SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key-here-min-32-chars
SESSION_SECRET=your-session-secret-here-min-32-chars

# Supabase (optional)
SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=xxx
```

### Keeping .env.example Updated

- Update `.env.example` when adding new variables
- Include all required variables
- Use placeholder values
- Document optional variables
- Group related variables with comments

## Security Best Practices

### Never Commit Secrets

```gitignore
# .gitignore
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
```

### Secret Management

- Use environment variables for secrets
- Never hardcode secrets in code
- Use different secrets for each environment
- Rotate secrets regularly
- Use secret management services for production

### Client-Side Variables

- Only expose non-sensitive data with `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
- Never expose API keys, secrets, or tokens
- Consider security implications of exposed variables

